# How this course was made (W3C PROV-O): written from the app's code and
# documentation, then checked in the review rounds below.
<#compilation>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-09T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:used <https://github.com/solid-memo/website> ,
              <https://solid-memo.com/> ,
              <http://augmentingcognition.com/ltm.html> ,
              <https://docs.ankiweb.net/studying.html> ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith <#anton-wiklund> ;
    prov:wasInformedBy <#review-0> ,
                       <#review-1> ,
                       <#review-2> ,
                       <#review-3> ,
                       <#review-4> ,
                       <#review-5> ,
                       <#review-6> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "About this record: the course was planned by AI agents from an inventory of the app's user-facing features, read from its code and its English and Swedish texts, and from research on how to introduce an app and spaced repetition to newcomers; three outlines were drafted from different angles and merged into one, and its chapters were then written in English and Swedish. The working notes and the outline are kept with the course's sources, not in the library."@en ,
                 "The course was checked by machine (the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators, its course and Markdown checks) and by AI agents against the app's code; it has had no human review."@en ,
                 "Selection: what a newcomer needs first, in the order they meet it: studying and honest grading, where their study is kept (a Solid Pod, or the browser as a guest) and how to keep guest study, the deck library and courses, making their own decks and cards, and pace and progress. Left out on purpose: screens a beginner rarely meets (data checks and repair, format updates, setting up storage), the scheduling formula's details and exact intervals, and the names of buttons and pages beyond a few, since questions test what a feature does and how it behaves, not where it sits on the screen."@en ,
                 "Structure: 5 chapters of 12 steps in all. Each step is a short theory text and one or two multiple-choice questions on it; each chapter has one review question that combines its steps and is asked only in its final review. Every question has one correct option (the card's back) and three wrong options (its distractors) of similar length and form, each with a note on why it is wrong. Every text is in English and Swedish."@en ,
                 "Licensing: the course is CC0 1.0, like most of the library. Its theory, questions, options and notes describe Solid Memo, whose code and documentation are the compiler's own (MIT License, copyright Anton Wiklund), in the course's own words. The two essays on spaced repetition below were used for facts only: nothing was copied from them."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-09T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Machine checks"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: the whole release, with the library checker (npm run library:check), which validates it against Solid Memo's shapes (library deck format 5, card format 5, chapter, step and distractor format 1), DCAT-AP and SKOS, with the reference data, checks the course's outline by its rules and its Markdown by the rules for a release."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The release conforms to every shape, profile and rule the checker applies to it."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-09T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Outline: facts and completeness (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: the merged outline, each chapter's theory points, answers and wrong options checked by an AI agent against the app's code, which tried to refute each one, and the outline as a whole by another for what a newcomer would miss or be lost by; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 29 problems were found, among them wrong options that were true in some cases, answers that held only after a lapse, and claims about a backup the app does not offer; the outline was revised and cut to 26 questions before the chapters were written."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-09T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Chapter text: facts, Swedish and questions (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: each chapter as written, reviewed by three AI agents: one checking every fact, answer and wrong option against the app's code, one the Swedish text against the app's own Swedish terms, and one the questions' design and the release's format rules; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: the findings (such as a wrong option that held in one case, notes that overstated a rule, options whose form gave the answer away, and calques in the Swedish) were fixed in the text, or kept with a reason."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-09T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Course as a whole (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: the five chapters read together by an AI agent, in both languages, for terms used differently, contradictions, facts explained twice, and theory that relies on a later chapter; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: a few terms were aligned with the app's labels, a step that repeated an earlier one was shortened, and two notes were corrected to say a reset is per deck."@en .

<#review-4>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-09T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Recheck against the app's later changes (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: every chapter, in both languages, checked again by AI agents against the app's code after it changed how guest study is kept (added to an instance one has, rather than always moved into a new one), how instances are updated and deleted, and how the deck list offers the library's course for newcomers, then the edits checked once more by another; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: the step on keeping guest study, the questions on it and on deleting an instance, and a few sentences on courses and daily limits were rewritten to match; the other chapters held as they were."@en .

<#review-5>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-10T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Version 2: theory in chunks (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: every step's theory, in both languages, split by an AI agent into chunks read one at a time, with a Markdown thematic break where its topic turns and at the same place in English and Swedish; the release compared by a script with version 1, which found the breaks and the release's own metadata the only changes, and checked by the library checker (no empty chunk, as many chunks in each language); not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: each of the 12 steps is read in two chunks; no text was reworded, and every chapter, step, card and distractor keeps its id."@en .

<#review-6>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-10T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Version 3: title, description and keywords in Korean, German, Spanish and French (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: the deck’s title, description and keywords, translated by an AI agent from the English (the Swedish settling what was ambiguous) into Korean, German, Spanish and French, in the words the app itself uses, and checked by a second AI agent that translated every text back into English and fixed what had drifted; the release compared by a script with version 2, which found the new title, description and keywords and the release’s own metadata the only changes, and checked by the library checker; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: the title, description and keywords are in Korean, German, Spanish and French as well; no card, chapter, step or distractor changed."@en .

